# What a published sdist contains, decided rather than inherited.
#
# `[tool.setuptools.packages.find] include = ["boty*"]` in pyproject.toml decides
# what goes in the WHEEL. It constrains the sdist not at all. Measured: an sdist
# built from this tree with no manifest contained `boty/*.py`, `README.md`,
# `pyproject.toml`, `PKG-INFO`, a generated `setup.cfg`, `bot_y.egg-info/*` — and
# every single `tests/test_*.py`, while shipping neither `tests/conftest.py`, nor
# `tests/fixtures/`, nor `config/products.yaml`, nor `scripts/`. That is a test
# suite published to PyPI that cannot possibly run: worse than shipping none,
# because it looks like coverage.
#
# PRUNE RATHER THAN GRAFT, and that is the decision. The graft-it-all fix would
# make the suite runnable from an sdist, and it would also put captured retailer
# HTML into a public artifact. This repo redacts fixtures by CLASS rather than by
# value precisely because a by-value guard passed on a Target capture carrying a
# session token, a visitor id, Akamai's geolocation of this host and five nearby
# store addresses. A file set that never leaves the repository is the stronger
# guarantee, and `pip install` does not need the tests.
#
# Only `prune tests` removes anything today. The other eight are guards, and they
# are here rather than omitted because the failure mode is silent: a future
# `graft`, an `include_package_data` flip or a `data_files` entry can pull any of
# these directories into a published artifact with nothing printed. Adding a
# top-level directory to this repo now forces a decision about whether it ships —
# `tests/test_packaging_metadata.py::test_every_unpackaged_top_level_directory_is_pruned_from_the_sdist`
# goes red until a line appears here.
#
# There are deliberately NO `exclude` lines for `Makefile`, `docker-compose.yml`,
# `QUESTIONS.md` or `state.json`. Measured: `prune` naming a directory that never
# entered is silent, but `exclude` naming a file that never entered prints
# `warning: no previously-included files found matching '...'` on every build,
# forever. A permanent warning trains everyone to stop reading build output.
# The one `include` in this file, and it is the opposite direction to everything
# below it. setuptools' own sdist defaults already carry `README.md`, `LICENSE`
# and `pyproject.toml`; `CHANGELOG.md` is not among them. An sdist is what a
# distribution packager builds from, and the changelog is part of the source of
# record — the same argument the `Changelog` URL in `[project.urls]` makes to a
# reader of the metadata.
include CHANGELOG.md

prune tests
prune config
prune docs
prune scripts
prune deploy
prune hooks
prune served
# `.github` arrived with the CI workflow, and it is the first new top-level
# directory since the rule above landed. The gate fired on it exactly as
# designed — `['.github']`, named in the assertion message — before this line was
# written. CI configuration describes how THIS repository is checked; it is of no
# use to anyone who ran `pip install bot-y`, and a workflow file in an sdist is
# one more place a reader could mistake for something they are meant to run.
prune .github
prune .planning
